{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And make atonement for yourself and for the people.</b> The atonement of Aaron is doubled: one corresponding to what he himself sinned, and a second corresponding to what he also caused the people to sin, as it says (Exodus 32:21): ״כִּי הֵבֵאתָ עָלָיו חֲטָאָה גְדוֹלָה״. But in the atonement of the people it says only ״וְכַפֵּר בַּעֲדָם״, for they are dependent on him, and he is not dependent on them. And furthermore, the atonement of Aaron is essentially the atonement of the people, for when the leaders of Israel are rectified then the entire people is rectified, and their corruption is the guilt of the people, as it says (Leviticus 4:3): ״אִם הַכֹּהֵן הַמָּשִׁיחַ יֶחֱטָא לְאַשְׁמַת הָעָם״, for an inadvertent error in learning counts as intentional, and when he sins even inadvertently, then his inadvertence becomes the cause of the people's guilt, that they too become guilty intentionally. Therefore his atonement is the atonement of the entire people." "Another interpretation: ״וּבְעַד הָעָם״ refers to the offering of the people, and the reason it is mentioned here in connection with his atonement is to tell you that the atonement of Aaron depends on the atonement of the people — that if the people attain atonement, then you too have atonement; but if the people do not attain atonement, then you too have no atonement or rectification. For anyone who causes the multitudes to sin — the sin of the multitudes hangs upon him, and he is not empowered to do repentance, so that it not come to pass that the teacher is in the Garden of Eden while his disciple is in Gehenna. Therefore he has no atonement except on this condition: that he also make atonement for the people whom he caused to sin." "And some say that Aaron did not need atonement except for the sin of deed and not for the sin of thought, as explained above in the verse ״קַח לְךָ עֵגֶל״, and the people also needed atonement for the sin of deed. Therefore in the atonement of Aaron it says ״וְכַפֵּר בַּעַדְךָ וּבְעַד הָעָם״, to tell you that the people too need your atonement for the sin of deed. But regarding the atonement of the people, who brought a calf as a burnt offering to atone also for the sin of thought, it says only ״וְכַפֵּר בַּעֲדָם״ alone, for Aaron did not need atonement for the thought, for he did not sin in thought at all but only in deed, and without doubt he did not intend it for the sake of divinity."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:11.108715116Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}